/bin/
/testdata/spec/
/third_party/
*.out

# benchstat inputs. `make bench` names its log after the rev it measured, because a fixed
# name is how one arm's log became the other's (grave #612); new.txt and old.txt stay listed
# so a log left behind by the old target is still ignored rather than newly committable.
bench-*.txt
new.txt
old.txt

# Fuzz *cache* is machine-local and disposable. Note the deliberate asymmetry:
# internal/*/testdata/fuzz/ crashers are COMMITTED — a crasher we found is a
# regression test we authored, and it belongs in history (decision 0005). Only
# the upstream spec corpora are gitignored, because we did not write those.
/testdata/fuzz-cache/

# Upstream component-model Canonical ABI model, vendored at the pin for the offline fixture generator (ADR 0084); never committed.
internal/component/canon/gen/vendor/
__pycache__/

# `make ci` writes its own verdict here — exit code, the HEAD SHA it ran on, dirtiness, timestamp — because a
# shell's return code cannot be trusted to carry it: `;` hands the status to whatever ran last, and a
# background task's notification carries the wrapper's code. `prmerge.sh` refuses to merge unless this file's
# SHA is the local tip and its exit is 0. Untracked: it is a fact about one machine's last run.
.ci-verdict

# The gate lock: one `make ci` at a time. Holder pid, process group and start SHA, so a reader can identify a
# holder before ending it. Untracked — it is a fact about one machine's current run.
.ci-lock

# Cargo build directories under the async-lift guests. The COMPONENTS are committed and the build script is
# run deliberately, out of CI (ADR 0086's "commit the oracle's reading" shape) — so `target/` is a by-product
# of regenerating them, not an input. Scoped to this testdata path rather than a bare `target/`: this is a Go
# module with no Rust of its own, and a tree-wide rule would quietly ignore a future directory that matters.
internal/component/testdata/asynclift/*/target/

# The edit-route hook's refusal log: when, which rule, a hash of the command. Untracked because it is a fact
# about one machine's recent sessions, and because the point is to measure the hook's FALSE-POSITIVE rate
# rather than to publish a project-wide statistic. `scripts/refusals.sh` reports it.
.editroute-log
